{ignore}
<p>A <code>{block 'name'}</code> pair plays one of two roles, depending on where it stands.
In a layout or a directory's <code>_inits.pad</code>/<code>_exits.pad</code> it is a
<em>region</em>: its content is the default that renders unless something overrides it. A
block standing directly in a page's own template - not inside another tag - is an
<em>override</em>: it replaces the region of that name in the page's frame, the layout it
<a href="?tag/extends"><code>{extends}</code></a> or its directories' wrappers. Inside an
override, <a href="?tag/parent"><code>{parent}</code></a> is the content it replaces, so a
page can add to a region instead of replacing it.</p>

<p>Like <code>{extends}</code>, the blocks are resolved in the text while the page is
assembled and are gone before it renders, so they cost nothing at render time. This lets a
page without any PHP set its wrapper's title: <code>{block 'title'}Sales{/block}</code>
replaces the <code>{block 'title'}...{/block}</code> in the <code>&lt;title&gt;</code> of
<code>_inits.pad</code>. A block of a page that overrides nothing stays where it stands and
renders its content.</p>

<p>The name must be quoted: a <code>{block}</code> without one (the <code>_common</code>
snippet of that name) is not a layout block. A block that reaches the render - one in a
snippet or a custom tag's template, where nothing overrides it - simply renders its content.
Under the strict check, a block of an extending page that overrides no block of the layout is
an error, as is a block that never closes. What stands between <code>&#123;ignore&#125;</code>
tags is left alone.</p>
{/ignore}
